course: physics
lesson: 36
title: Speed: fast and slow
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Speed is how far you go in a certain time: when the car's speedometer shows 50, the car would cover 50 kilometres if it kept that speed for a whole hour.
explain: Moving things keep moving the way they are moving until something stops or turns them; this is called inertia, and it is why you lurch forward when the car brakes.
explain: On a smooth train a ball you drop lands at your feet, because the ball was already moving along with the train and you.
explain: To speed up, slow down or turn, you need a push: the engine pushes the car faster, the brakes slow it, and the ground pushes sideways on your feet when you turn.
explain: A fast car carries much more energy than a slow one, so it needs a much longer way to stop, and that is why the speed limit outside a school is low.
fact: Speed is how far something goes in a certain time; 50 kilometres an hour means 50 kilometres in one hour at that speed.
fact: To compare speeds you need both the distance and the time; the winner of a race covers the distance in the least time.
fact: A cheetah can sprint at about 100 kilometres an hour for a short time, while a snail takes a few minutes to go a metre.
fact: Moving things keep moving the way they are going until something stops or turns them; this is inertia.
fact: A ball dropped on a smooth train lands at your feet because it was already moving with the train.
fact: When a car brakes, your body keeps moving forward; when it turns sharply, you lurch outwards.
fact: A quick pull on a tablecloth can leave the dishes behind because they are slow to start moving, but a slow pull drags them with it.
fact: A lighter car speeds up faster than a heavy lorry with the same engine.
fact: At twice the speed, a car needs about four times the braking distance.
fact: Before braking even starts, a driver needs a moment to see, think and press the pedal, and the car carries on at full speed meanwhile; a phone makes that moment longer.
fact: A passenger plane flies at about 900 kilometres an hour, many times faster than a car, yet high in the sky it is so far away that it seems to crawl.
fact: To turn, you need a sideways push from the ground on your feet, which is why footballers slip turning on wet grass.
exercise: A car goes steadily at 50 kilometres an hour. How far does it go in half an hour? | 25 kilometres | 50 kilometres | 100 kilometres | 5 kilometres
exercise: What is speed? | How far you go in a certain time | How heavy something is when it moves | How long a journey takes in all | How hard the engine pushes
exercise: What do you need to know to compare two runners' speeds? | The distance and the time | Only their shoe sizes and ages | Only their heights and weights | Only the distance they both run
exercise: Who wins a race over the same distance? | The one who takes the least time | The one who takes the most steps | The one who starts last | The one with the longest legs
exercise: About how fast can a cheetah sprint? | About 100 kilometres an hour | About 10 kilometres an hour | About 1,000 kilometres an hour | About 30 kilometres an hour
exercise: Why does a ball dropped on a smooth train land at your feet? | It is already moving with the train | The train pulls it down with magnets | The air in the carriage holds it in place | It falls too fast to move backwards
exercise: Why do you lurch forward when a car brakes? | Your body keeps moving forward as the car slows | The seat springs push you forward as the car stops | The brakes pull everything towards the front of the car | The air inside the car rushes forward and pushes you
exercise: What is the name for the way moving things keep moving? | Inertia | Gravity | Friction | Magnetism
exercise: Why must a tablecloth be pulled fast for the trick to work? | The dishes are slow to start moving, so a quick pull leaves them | A fast pull makes the dishes lighter | The air holds the dishes down | A fast pull makes the cloth slippery
exercise: Why does a light car speed up faster than a heavy lorry with the same engine? | The same push changes a lighter thing's speed more | The car's wheels are smaller, so they spin round quicker | Lorries have weaker brakes | The car is closer to the ground
exercise: How much longer is the braking distance at twice the speed? | About four times longer | Exactly the same distance | Just twice as long as before | Only half as long as before
exercise: Why are speed limits low near schools? | Slower cars can stop in a much shorter distance | Children like watching slow cars | Fast cars make too much noise for children in lessons | The roads there are made of softer stuff
exercise: Why does the car still travel a bit after the driver sees a ball run into the road? | It takes time to see, think and press the brake | The brakes only start to work after a few seconds | The ball pushes the car forward | Cars cannot stop in a straight line
exercise: Why is using a phone while driving banned? | Drivers react even later, so the car goes further before braking | Phones make the engine go faster | The phone signal confuses the brakes | It wastes the car's battery
exercise: About how fast does a passenger plane fly? | About 900 kilometres an hour | About 90 kilometres an hour | About 9,000 kilometres an hour | About 50 kilometres an hour
exercise: Why do footballers slip when they turn fast on wet grass? | There is not enough grip for the sideways push they need | Wet grass pulls their feet backwards | Water makes their legs heavier | Rain makes the ball spin
test: bro said if u drop a ball on a moving train it'll fly to the back. true?? | No, it lands at your feet; it is moving with the train | Yes, the train moves away from it | Yes, it shoots backwards really fast as the train speeds on | No, it floats in the air
test: Why does a plane high in the sky look like it is crawling, though it goes about 900 kilometres an hour? | It is so far away that it seems to move slowly | It really does fly slowly when it is up that high | The clouds and the thin air hold it back up there | It is actually going backwards against the strong wind
test: In a cartoon, a car brakes hard and the passengers are thrown backwards. What's wrong? | They would lurch forward, because their bodies keep moving | Nothing, braking throws you backwards | They would float upwards | They would stay perfectly still
test: Uncle Joe says driving twice as fast only needs twice the stopping distance. Put him right. | It needs about four times the distance | He is right, it is exactly twice as far | It needs the very same distance as before | It needs only half the distance, since you go faster
test: Ella wants to try the tablecloth trick with Gran's best plates. What's the sensible advice? | Practise with plastic cups; a slow pull drags dishes off | Go ahead, a slow careful pull is safest | Go ahead, it always works | Use a tablecloth that is wet
test: When a car turns sharply left, which way does your body lurch? | Outwards, to the right | Inwards, to the left side | Straight down into the seat | Backwards, towards the boot
test: A snail goes a metre in a few minutes. Which is true? | A cheetah could cover the same metre in a blink | A snail is faster than a person walking along the pavement | A snail never moves at all | Snails go about 100 kilometres an hour
test: just shut up and drive, I'm texting, it's fine. is it? | No, drivers on phones react later and travel further before braking | Yes, phones make drivers react faster | Yes, if the car is slow | Yes, texting doesn't count as driving
test: Two runners both run 100 metres. Ana takes 15 seconds, Ben takes 18. Who is faster? | Ana | Ben | They are equally fast | You cannot tell without their weight
test: What would happen if you ran straight on ice and tried to turn sharply? | Your feet would slide, with no grip to push you round | You would turn more easily than on grass | You would stop at once | You would fly upwards
test: A full lorry and an empty one have the same engine. Which speeds up faster? | The empty lorry | The full lorry | Both the same | Neither can speed up
exam: Identify the quantities which must be known in order to determine the speed of a moving body. | The distance travelled and the time taken | The mass of the body together with its colour | The time taken alone | The height of the body above the ground
exam: Ascertain the approximate factor by which the braking distance of a vehicle increases when its speed is doubled. | Approximately fourfold | Approximately twofold | It remains unaltered | It is reduced by one half
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the forward motion of a passenger when a vehicle decelerates abruptly? | The body of the passenger tends to continue in its state of motion | The seat exerts a forward force upon the passenger | The brakes attract the passenger towards the front | The air contained within the vehicle propels the passenger forwards
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the use of telephones by drivers. | It delays the reaction of the driver and so lengthens the distance travelled before braking | It enhances the alertness of the driver | It has no bearing whatsoever upon stopping distance | It causes the brakes of the vehicle to fail
